@begin(section)

@title(Quickstart)

This is a five-minute tour: initialize a project, install a package,
look at what you got, verify it, and remove it.  Every command here
works against a real zick build — run them in a scratch directory to
follow along.

@begin(section)
@title(Set Up a Project)

Make a directory and initialize it:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
mkdir my-project
cd my-project
zick init
@end(code)

@c(zick init) creates the @c(.zick-db) marking directory and the
store.  zick finds the project by walking up from wherever you run it,
looking for that directory — so from now on, running zick anywhere
inside @c(my-project) (or below it) talks to this project.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Install a Package)

A package is a zip archive at a URL.  Install one with @c(zick add),
naming it with @c(-k), versioning it with @c(-V), and locating it with
@c(-l):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k mylib -V 1.2.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip
@end(code)

zick downloads the archive into a staging area, unpacks it into the
project tree, and records the package and every file it owns — path,
size, and SHA-256 checksum — in the store.  Success looks like:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    result successful
    status successful
}
@end(code)

Declare that one package depends on another with @c(-u) (the dependee
must already be installed):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k myapp -V 0.1.0 -l https://example.com/myapp.zip -u mylib
@end(code)

@c(-W) skips the download and records the package without unpacking
its files — useful for metadata-only records, or for following along
offline.  (The archive URLs in this tour are examples; substitute
real archives you can download.)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Look at What You Got)

See what is installed:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick list
@end(code)

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    packages [
        {
            location "https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip"
            metadata [
            ]
            name "mylib"
            version "1.2.0"
        }
        {
            location "https://example.com/myapp.zip"
            metadata [
            ]
            name "myapp"
            version "0.1.0"
        }
    ]
    result successful
    status successful
}
@end(code)

See the files a package owns:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick files -k mylib
@end(code)

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    package-files [
        {
            checksum "f5804ac61aa4b37500ea52077f984d7224a35d3e2d05644d62ac4940384cfa6e"
            class normal-file
            path "mylib/bin/tool"
            size 44
        }
    ]
    result package-found
    status successful
}
@end(code)

Ask who depends on whom:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick dependers -k mylib
zick dependees -k myapp
@end(code)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Verify)

Check that the files on disk still match what was installed:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick verify -k myapp
@end(code)

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    result package-found
    status successful
    verification-results [
    ]
}
@end(code)

zick exits 0 when everything checks out, 3 when the package is not
installed, and 4 when verification finds problems (with the failing
files listed under @c(verification-results)).  Try deleting one of the
installed files and re-running @c(verify) to see the report — that is
what a @c(git clean) or a bad deploy looks like to zick.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Remove)

Uninstall a package with @c(zick remove).  Its files are deleted (its
configuration files are backed up first) and its record leaves the
store:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick remove -k myapp
@end(code)

Removing a package that others depend on is an error unless you pass
@c(-c) to cascade, removing the dependents too.  Preview a removal
without touching anything with @c(-r):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick remove -k mylib -c -r
@end(code)

And when you want to find the cruft — packages nothing else depends
on — ask zick:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick orphans
@end(code)

@end(section)

